# Horo Extension SDK @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@

This self-contained package is the public C ABI surface for native Horo
extensions. It has no compile-time engine or third-party dependencies and does
not require an engine source checkout.

Point `HoroEngineExtensionSdk_DIR` at
`lib/cmake/HoroEngineExtensionSdk`, then consume the header-only target:

```cmake
find_package(HoroEngineExtensionSdk @HORO_EXTENSION_SDK_VERSION@ CONFIG REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my_extension PRIVATE HoroEngine::ExtensionSdk)
```

Create a portable starter project with:

```text
python bin/horo-scaffold-extension.py --shape backend --id com.example.tools --name "Example Tools" --output example-tools
```

Supported shapes are `gui`, `backend`, `script`, and `hybrid`. Generated
projects contain their manifest, C ABI entry modules, contract tests, and CPack
ZIP packaging configuration.

The SDK supports `horo-extension` host ABI versions from
@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MIN_HOST_MINOR@
(inclusive) to @HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAX_HOST_MAJOR_EXCLUSIVE@.0 (exclusive), and
publishes declarations through ABI
@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_MAJOR_VERSION@.@HORO_EXTENSION_ABI_PUBLISHED_MINOR@.
Extensions declare the exact minimum host minor they require through
`horo_extension_query`; incompatible hosts reject the module before load.

## Source-free extension author CI

`share/horo/extension-sdk/ci/extension-author-ci.yml` is the versioned GitHub
Actions template. The SDK scaffolder copies it into each generated project's
`.github/workflows/extension-ci.yml`, with a bootstrap script and
`.horo/extension-ci.lock.json`. The lock must stay at that path, and CI writes
only to the author project's `ci-artifacts/` directory. Authors pin an HTTPS SDK ZIP and its SHA-256 for
**each** Linux x64, macOS arm64, macOS x64 and Windows x64 job. The ZIP must contain this
SDK package's `bin/`, `lib/`, `include/` and `share/` directories at its root.
The bootstrap refuses unpinned, mismatched, oversized or unsafe archives and
checks the downloaded SDK version against the lock. The author repository needs
no engine source checkout. The workflow pins action commits, Python and CMake
versions, uses read-only permissions, and never receives signing credentials.
The SDK CI runner parses bounded CTest JUnit input with DTD and entity declarations
disabled; it has no extra Python package dependency.

The SDK's `bin/horo-extension-author-ci.py` then configures and builds the
project, runs all CTest tests, installs the package root, validates
`extension.json`, checks every native module's ABI, packs a canonical
`.horopkg`, and verifies archive integrity. It publishes the package, JUnit
test output and a provenance JSON containing repository, source commit, SDK
version and digest, platform and package digest. The CI trust policy permits
unsigned *integrity* verification only; it does not attest publisher identity,
grant runtime trust, or sign a release. A release pipeline must separately sign
with an approved protected key and verify against publisher trust policy. The
package tool does not yet validate full typed TOML semantics or activation.

Custom projects using this template must install `extension.json`,
`horo-package.toml`, and every manifest-declared native binary to the package
root. The scaffold demonstrates that layout. Keep SDK URLs/digests in the
version-controlled lock, and update them deliberately for an SDK upgrade.

See `share/horo/extension-sdk/extension-sdk.json` for machine-readable package
metadata. Validate a manifest without loading extension code with:

```text
bin/horo-extension-validate --json --schema-version 1 extension.json
```

The matching authoring schema is
`share/horo/extension-sdk/extension-manifest-v1.schema.json`. The validator is
the behavioral authority and reports stable codes plus exact JSON field paths.

Exercise a trusted native module's ABI negotiation, host callback ownership,
result-table shape, and unload path with:

```text
bin/horo-extension-conformance --json path/to/native-module
```

The harness reports stable stage-specific codes and exits nonzero for an
incompatible module. It loads native code in the harness process, so run it only
against trusted build output.

The SDK also includes the versioned `bin/horo-package` author tool. It needs no
engine source checkout. `--version` reports the command contract version.

```text
bin/horo-package pack <package-root> <output.horopkg>
bin/horo-package inspect <output.horopkg>
bin/horo-package sign <output.horopkg> --publisher com.example.studio --key-id release-1 --key <unencrypted-p256.pem> --output <signature.json>
bin/horo-package verify <output.horopkg> --package-id com.example.package --trust <trust.json> --signature <signature.json>
```

`pack` requires `horo-package.toml` in a real directory and creates a canonical
`files.manifest.json`; it rejects symlinks, noncanonical paths and existing output.
File order and ZIP metadata are deterministic for identical inputs and this tool
version. The output must be outside the package root. Keep credentials outside
the package root: the packer rejects recognized private-key PEM content but
cannot recognize arbitrary secret content. `sign`
accepts an unencrypted P-256 PEM private key readable only by its owner on POSIX,
never writes or prints its contents, and creates a detached v1 JSON envelope.
Do not pass a private key as a command-line argument. Store it in an approved
credential system and materialize a short-lived owner-only file when necessary.

`verify` applies the same bounded archive and publisher-signature policy used by
the current package restore path. The caller supplies the package ID and an
explicit trust-policy JSON document; no author-declared key becomes trusted by
itself. Trust JSON v1 has `schemaVersion`, `allowUnsigned`, and `publishers`;
each publisher has `publisherId`, `keyId`, `algorithm` (`ecdsa-p256-sha256`),
`publicKeyHex` (uncompressed P-256 point), `expiresAtUnixMilliseconds` (0 means
no expiry), and `revoked`. Omit `--signature` only when the trust policy explicitly
allows unsigned packages. The command verifies archive structure and content,
not yet-unimplemented typed TOML package semantics or extension activation.
